invariant: INV-16
file: sha512_hw.c
catches: test/docker-aes-runtime-qemu.sh sha2-equiv
reason: the finalize of sha512_hw.c's arm64 arm writes zero as the upper
  word of the length where FIPS 180-4 §5.1.2 writes the upper 64 bits of
  the bit count. Every message under 2^61 bytes has the same digest, so
  the published vectors and the Wycheproof suites pass on the
  instructions. CBMC cannot read an intrinsic, so no proof covers this
  arm, and bin/sha2_equiv_test is what holds it: it starts a context at a
  byte count of 2^61 - 1 and at larger ones and compares the digest with
  one whose padding the test writes from a 128-bit product
  (test/sha2_equiv_length512.h). This arm of the file is arm64's, so the
  catch is the script, which builds the binary for arm64 and runs it under
  qemu-aarch64, whose max model has the instructions on every host. The
  lane skips without docker, and this then reports unguarded rather than
  caught.

--- old
// big-endian integer, written into the pending block as sha512.c's finalize writes it.
static void finalize(sha512 *s) {
    uint64_t high = s->total_bytes >> 61;
--- new
// big-endian integer, written into the pending block as sha512.c's finalize writes it.
static void finalize(sha512 *s) {
    uint64_t high = 0;
